i'm an engineer by background and came  to stanford for business school where i  met uh met sam became  friends uh started chatting about  different ideas what we might do after  business school and yeah we hatched the  idea of actually starting a solo  installation business at the time  focused on emerging markets  where energy is a big issue in terms of  access reliability and cost and so we  thought solar would be a great  great solution for that during our  summer of  business school we  put together this pilot project which  was a boarding school in nairobi kenya  that the whole thing souped to nuts  found the project designed it sourced  suppliers financed the system flew down  there and installed it the system was a  was a great success uh but  we also you know um realized how  cumbersome the whole process was of  going of going soul of designing solar  we were meeting nights and weekends uh  out of our dorm rooms in palo alto  trying to design the optimal solar  installation for a site halfway across  the world  and part of the challenges there were  first of all it was our first time doing  anything of the scale second of all we  weren't on site we had to hire people  locally to go and take a bunch of  measurements for us take a bunch of  photographs and we'll get all that  information back to our dorm rooms after  our classes were done and then try and  put together a solar installation that  would work it did take us essentially a  year  to do something that we thought should  be much easier much faster and it's  something that a lot of other people go  through  [Music]  if you look at any solar professional  they're essentially three key things  that they need to do in order to come up  with a solar installation for a property  first thing they need to do is be able  to figure out how many solar panels will  fit on the property the second thing  they need to do is forecast how much  energy those solar panels will produce  the third thing they need to do is how  much figure out how much money that will  save the customer  what aurora does is it streamlines the  entire process of answering those three  questions  when we were reviewing companies for  this year's ai 50 list aurora solar  jumped out because it was one of the  most interesting applications of ai  technology typically we see ai being  used for lots of things like  self-driving cars a whole range of  office and business services  cyber security things like that but what  aurora solar is doing is a little bit  unique where  they are using these advantages that you  can get from advanced ai  to custom design  solar power systems that on rooftops  that might ordinarily be a little too  difficult  to put solar on  the process of designing an optimal flow  insulation and doing that in an  efficient timely manner is actually  really difficult there's many different  steps you have to  run through many things you can optimize  right like where you place the panels  depending on the roof geometry depending  on the  shading that might be from a tree or so  forth different design configurations  and so  machine learning  and the optimization tools that we  developed allows us to automate the  entire process of designing a solo  installation it wasn't easy in the early  days back when we started it was just we  came out of the wake of the first  cleantech boom  a lot of people had lost a lot of money  you know investing in technologies that  didn't work out and so  solo was not a not a hot thing back then  the first five years of the company's  life we basically grew the company on  what is a very relatively small amount  in funding  for a company based out of silicon  valley but then within the last three  years we've had a tremendous amount of  traction  to date the company estimates it's  designed nearly 8 000 individual  projects  for about 7 000  residential and commercial customers  it's raised more than half a billion  dollars  including a 200 million funding round  announced in early 2022  and presently  it's valued at 4.4 billion dollars  according to a pitch book estimate wind  and solar together can be almost half of  our energy mix by 2050 and so it's an  incredible transformation that's  happening and so it was clear to us like  to get from where we were then and even  where we are today to that future there  needed to be a streamlined process and a  data-driven way of designing uh and  installing and deploying these solar  installations at scale i would say that  the one metric we've always tracked this  is from before we had any customers and  when we're just first building the  product and continues to be true till  today  was the number of projects that is  addresses that our software is being  used to design solar for right  and i still remember the big milestone  of the point where we got to a thousand  addresses a week right we couldn't it  was like this is amazing a thousand  addresses a week are going through our  software and being designed by it  more recently it's a hundred thousand a  week and we want to get to the point  where it's a hundred thousand per day so  those are the milestones that keep us  going and yes the funding valuation all  those things enable us to get there but  that's ultimately the real goal that  we're going for
 

